<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amar Singh I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1559">1559</a>–<a href="/years/1620">1620</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Amar Singh I of Mewar · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia-dynasty">Sisodia dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amar Singh I was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana">Maharana</a> of Mewar from 1597 to 1620, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana-pratap">Maharana Pratap</a>. After years of war against Mughal armies, he made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a> in 1615 on honourable terms: Mewar accepted Mughal overlordship, but the maharana was excused personal attendance at the Mughal court.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amar-singh-i.jpg" alt="Amar Singh I" width="320" height="503" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raja Ravi Varma · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raja_Ravi_Varma,_Maharana_Amar_Singh_-_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Amar Singh was born in the old capital fortress of Chittor on 16 March 1559 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana-pratap">Maharana Pratap</a>'s chief queen, Ajabde Punwar, who belonged to the Parmarji of Bijolia, a fiefdom under Mewar. In the same year his grandfather, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udai-singh-ii">Udai Singh II</a>, laid the foundation of the new capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a>. By the end of his reign Pratap had recovered most of the ancestral kingdom, but Chittor and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalgarh">Mandalgarh</a> remained in Mughal hands. Amar Singh succeeded him on his death on 19 January 1597, as the 14th Rana of Mewar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> soon sent a strong force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/man-singh-i">Raja Man Singh</a> and other commanders, nominally led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Prince Salim</a>. Following his father's policy, Amar Singh withdrew into the hills of western Mewar and raided Mughal outposts, and the campaign ended inconclusively when Man Singh was called to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. Akbar then planned to install Sagar, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udai-singh-ii">Udai Singh II</a>, as Rana at Chittor, but died before he could do so. After his accession Jahangir sent an army of 20,000 cavalry under the nominal command of Parviz Mirza, with Asaf Khan directing it on the emperor's orders, and Amar Singh fought a hard battle to defend his territory.</p><p>Further Mughal armies followed. Mahabat Khan's force of 1608 withdrew under constant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> raids, while Abdullah Khan stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chavand">Chavand</a>, where 2,000 to 3,000 Rajputs fought to the death, before he was beaten at the Battle of Ranakpur in 1611. In 1613 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a> came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputana">Rajputana</a> himself, with Prince Khurram leading the campaign on the ground, and in 1614 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> at last broke into the hills. Under the terms agreed in 1615 a relative of the Rana would attend the emperor in his place, Mewar would send 1,500 horsemen into Mughal service, and Chittor was returned on condition that its fort was never repaired.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Amar Singh was advised in the negotiations by his council and his grandmother, Jaiwanta Bai, and when he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a> he was warmly received. It is said that he felt the shame of accepting a Mughal farman so keenly that he withdrew to his private chambers, leaving the government to his heir, Karan Singh II. He died on 26 January 1620. He was given the title Chakraveer for his valour against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, and he patronised the author Mathuratmaja, whose works are sometimes attributed to him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amar Singh I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The relationship with the Mughal Empire was defined by the treaty of 1615, when <b>Amar Singh I</b> accepted Jahangir’s overlordship through Prince Khurram; Mewar was spared the marriage alliances made by other Rajput houses, the Maharana was excused personal attendance at court and his heir served in his…</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittor-fort">Chittorgarh Fort</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittor-fort">Chittorgarh Fort</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=24.89,74.65&amp;year=1598" aria-label="Chittorgarh Fort on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6531 1248 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Amar Singh I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6981 1518h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6981 1518h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=24.89,74.65&amp;year=1598">What was here in 1598? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
